KW  - Tutorial participation
KW  - Student engagement
AB  - Student attendance in higher education has declined substantially in recent years, yet little is known about how attendance patterns differ across teaching formats. This study provides a systematic comparison of lecture and tutorial attendance patterns and examines how factors associated with absenteeism differ between these two pedagogical formats. Using original survey data collected in March 2025 from about 200 undergraduate economics students at a UK Russell Group university, we analyse the drivers of absenteeism, their variations across demographic groups, and students’ perceived value of attending lectures and tutorials. Our results reveal clear and persistent differences. Lecture absenteeism is strongly associated with access to recorded lectures, inconvenient scheduling, less engaging sessions, and high student workload. By contrast, tutorials benefit from exclusive content, interactive problem-solving, and opportunities to ask questions, all of which substantially reduce absenteeism. Our regression estimates show that intrinsic motivation, engaging teaching, and in-class interactive activities significantly decrease the probability of missing more than half of classes. Conversely, commuting difficulties, perceiving classes as not stimulating, and belief that one can pass the class without attending increase absenteeism. Student suggestions reinforce these findings, with calls for more interactive teaching, better scheduling, and closer alignment between teaching materials and assessments. These findings demonstrate that lectures and tutorials offer distinct educational value and suggest the need for attendance strategies to combine logistical support with purposeful pedagogy, while recognising diverse student needs.
