AB  - As higher education increasingly adopts blended learning, understanding how instructors provide online feedback is critical for informing pedagogically grounded feedback design and supporting instructors’ professional development in blended teaching. This study examined feedback practices in a university-wide blended course with 1370 undergraduates and 40 instructors. By integrating content analysis, sequence mining and clustering, and process mapping, the study presents a multidimensional view of how feedback is provided over time. A total of 520 instructor feedback messages and 49,955 actions within 1343 behavioral sessions were analyzed to investigate both semantic and behavioral patterns. The results showed that Didactic Conceptual feedback, characterized by content-rich explanatory responses, was the most frequently used, particularly in response to student confusion. Four behavioral trajectories were identified: Task Focused, Video Oriented, Reply and Like Driven, and Progress Centered. On the basis of these patterns, instructors were grouped into three roles: Highly Interactive, Contemplative, and Lurking. Highly Interactive instructors frequently provided concise didactic responses. Contemplative instructors, although contributing fewer messages, offered longer reflective feedback based on sustained engagement with course materials. Lurking instructors contributed minimal feedback, typically in the form of brief guidance. By integrating content analysis with trajectory-based behavioral analysis, this study offers a process-oriented perspective on how instructor feedback unfolds in a large-scale blended course.
