AB  - This study investigated the Conversational AI (CAI) dependence among 217 pre-service teachers, focusing on the disparities across gender. Using validated subscales—Uncontrollability, Withdrawal Symptoms, Mood Modification, and Negative Impact—the study employed independent samples t-tests to examine gender differences in CAI dependence. Results revealed that male pre-service teachers exhibited significantly higher Uncontrollability and Mood Modification, indicating greater reliance on CAI for emotional regulation and cognitive engagement. Generally, dependence was primarily behavioral and cognitive, with limited psychological or clinical symptoms, highlighting the role of academic and digital environments in shaping AI-related behaviors. Findings underscore the need for universal, curriculum-integrated interventions that promote digital literacy, critical thinking, and responsible AI use among all pre-service teachers. Targeted support may be particularly beneficial for males to develop healthier self-regulation strategies.
