AB  - Generative AI is poised to fundamentally reshape management education, yet the field remains constrained by entrenched path dependence and institutional inertia. Prior studies on educational technology and institutional change, while valuable, have often examined technological adoption or organizational transformation in isolation, lacking a framework that captures their dynamic, nonlinear interplay with cognitive shifts. This study addresses this gap by introducing and empirically validating a ‘Technology-Organization-Cognition’ (T-O-C) resonance lens. Employing a three-stage mixed-methods design—analyzing a global database of “AI + Management Education” practices (2020–2025) via LLM-assisted text mining, Exponential Random Graph Models (ERGM), and Multiverse Analysis—we identify three emergent paradigms: Cognitive Augmentation, Personalized Learning, and Practical Wisdom. Inferential network analysis demonstrates that nonlinear T-O-C resonance is the core engine for breaking institutional inertia, with its effect significantly surpassing that of any single or paired dimension, revealing cognitive transformation as the ‘keystone’ that activates technological affordances and legitimizes organizational strategies. By moving beyond prior, more compartmentalized accounts, this research provides a holistic, mechanism-based framework for understanding complex socio-technical transitions, offering both a novel methodological approach and a strategic roadmap for institutions navigating the AI era.
