AB  - This study shares findings from a two-year pilot at a large private university in which first-year writing courses included weekly instruction and guidance on writing with generative AI. Drawing on entrance and exit survey data from 221 student responses across fourteen different pilot sections, the research reveals student attitudes and practices regarding GAI tools like ChatGPT. Key findings suggest that pilot participants primarily use GAI for early-stage writing processes, such as brainstorming and summarizing, rather than generating final text, and that while students recognize GAI's potential to enhance efficiency and overcome writing challenges, many share concerns about its impact on learning and have nuanced beliefs about its usefulness for composing. The piloting instructional team shares insights from their experiences in the pilot, including measures taken to promote transparency, ethical awareness, and the safeguarding of process-oriented pedagogies. These insights inform strategies to align GAI adoption with best practices as articulated in the MLA-CCCC Joint Task Force Working Papers on Writing and AI.
