AB  - This study developed a new tool for detecting demographic effects to automate the identification of how age group and continent influence students’ perceptions of ChatGPT’s disadvantages. Data from 166 participants were analyzed, measured across 14 dependent variables and 2 independent variables. A one-way ANOVA showed that age group significantly affected ChatGPT disadvantage scores (F = 3.03, p < 0.05). Post-hoc Tukey HSD testing confirmed that the primary difference was between the 22–25 age group and those under 18, with the 22–25 group reporting significantly higher scores. Effect size analysis indicated that the differences between the 18–21 and 26–30 groups (d = 0.38, small-to-moderate) and between the 22–25 and 26–30 groups (d = 0.15, small) were negligible in practical terms. Additionally, an independent-samples t-test revealed a highly significant difference between continents (t = 4.35, p < 0.05), with Asian students (M = 3.46, SD = 0.32) scoring higher than European students (M = 3.13, SD = 0.30) in their perceptions of ChatGPT’s disadvantages. This continental difference was not only statistically significant but also practically meaningful, as reflected by a large effect size (Cohen’s d = 1.07). A user-friendly web application was developed to automate and visualize the demographic impact of ChatGPT disadvantage perceptions in real time. Overall, the findings suggest that both age and continent play an important role in shaping students’ views of ChatGPT’s disadvantages. To use ChatGPT effectively, students under 18 may require training or digital literacy instruction. Asian students, who reported more awareness of ChatGPT’s drawbacks, may be more critical users, whereas European students may benefit from more structured AI literacy training. Given the large effect size observed for continental differences, institutions hosting international students should prioritize culturally responsive AI literacy programs that address diverse perspectives.
