AB  - The rapid integration of artificial intelligence (AI) systems such as ChatGPT into education has raised questions over their motivational and psychological impacts. This study examined the relationships between academic motivation, perceptions of ChatGPT, actual usage, well-being, and attitudes toward professional psychological support among Vietnamese students. Data were collected from 1,056 participants; however, only 900 persons with prior experience using ChatGPT were analyzed. Academic motivation was assessed using the Short Academic Motivation Scale, attitudes and usage were analyzed through the TAME-ChatGPT framework, well-being was evaluated with the Mental Health Continuum–Short Form, and attitudes toward psychological assistance were gauged using the abbreviated version of the ATSPPH. Partial Least Squares Structural Equation Modeling (PLS-SEM) shown that academic motivation strongly forecasts attitudes toward ChatGPT (β = 0.621, p < .001) and actual usage (β = 0.402, p < .001). Attitudes significantly influenced usage (β = 0.315, p < .001) but did not have a direct effect on well-being (β = 0.042, p = .276). Usage was a significant predictor of well-being (β = 0.298, p < .001) and help-seeking (β = 0.215, p < .001), whereas well-being positively influenced help-seeking (β = 0.336, p < .001). The model explained 43.2% of the variance in consumption, 28.9% of the variance in well-being, and 39.7% of the variance in help-seeking. These findings highlight that ChatGPT functions as both an educational tool and a psychological resource, fostering resilience and openness to professional support.
