AB  - Group work based on cooperative learning activities is essential and highly valued by higher education policymakers and practitioners in Chinese higher education. However, little is known about Chinese university students’ experiences with group work, perceptions of group work, and specific interactions with others during these activities. This qualitative study, drawing on Collins’s Interaction Ritual Chain theory, explored the experiences and perceptions of 21 undergraduate students at Chinese universities. Four distinct types of group work interactions were identified: like-minded, relationship-dependent, loosely detached, and task-oriented. These interaction types can have significantly different effects under varying conditions. Students’ experiences and views of group work are diverse and influenced by their individual focus and emotion. Essentially, students’ interactions during group work are rational choices based on emotional logic, rational computational logic, or a mixture of both. The findings provide new insights into group work interactions in higher education and may represent practical insights that instructors can use to improve students’ satisfaction with group work. This study also highlights the importance of considering the feasibility, grouping patterns, and grading rules of group work. Instructors are encouraged to prioritize the mutual focus and shared mood among students engaged in group work to mitigate negative effects on student interactions.
