AB  - While technology-supported learning has traditionally facilitated aspects of higher-order thinking and creativity, emerging generative artificial intelligence (Gen AI) holds significant potential for further development in this area. However, whether Gen AI-assisted learning could effectively foster students’ creativity has not yet achieved a consensus. To answer this question, this paper adopted meta-analysis to investigate 32 relevant (quasi-)experimental studies and evaluate the potential effect of Gen AI-assisted learning on students’ creativity. Results revealed that Gen AI-assisted learning demonstrated an approximately moderate positive correlation with students' creativity (g = 0.420). Moderator analysis indicated that creativity improvement brought by Gen AI-assisted learning was more effective (a) in the fields of medical nursing and natural sciences, (b) when teaching procedural and mixed knowledge, (c) when embedding Gen AI in other learning platforms, (d) when learning intervention lasted between 1 and 4 weeks, (e) in collaborative learning, (f) for code generation, (g) in other regions (Africa and South America). These results contributed insights for advancing future studies and practices in leveraging Gen AI-assisted learning to foster students’ creativity.
